        "titulo": "Spelling out loud and the accented letters",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: in Brazil people spell names constantly: on the phone, at the bank, at a hotel, for a delivery. You say the letter names above, in order. STEP 2: a few letters have a different name from English: «e» is «é», «g» is «gê» (ZHAY), «h» is «agá», «j» is «jota», «r» is «érre», «x» is «xis» and «y» is «ípsilon». STEP 3: Portuguese also writes letters with marks, and each mark has a job: «á é í ó ú» show the stressed syllable; «â ê ô» show the stressed syllable and a closed sound; «ã õ» show a nasal vowel; «à» appears only in a contraction (you will see it later); «ç» (cê cedilha) is the sound «s» before a, o, u: «obrigado» is no help, but «açaí» is. STEP 4: to give your e-mail you say «arroba» for @ and «ponto» for the dot: «ana ponto silva arroba exemplo ponto com».",
        "comparacao": "English spelling often hides the sound; Portuguese spelling is far more regular. Once you know the rules (next sessions), you can read almost any word aloud correctly. Mind the letter names, which you will hear all the time: «g» is not «gee», «e» is not «ee», «i» is not «eye».",
